Faceclaw T3 fork additions (September 6, 2026)

When enabled, T3 sends reviewed prompts, project/model selections, and plan
answers to the paired private bridge. It receives project/session summaries,
transcripts, activity, and completion events. Bridge-hosted dictation sends
microphone PCM to that bridge while recording. On-device recognition is the
backup. Original Faceclaw cloud-provider settings continue to apply when those
providers are selected.

Requested Luna refinement sends the draft and editing instruction through the
bridge's existing Codex login to its model provider. Anthropic refinement uses
the original Faceclaw API configuration. Refinement is never automatic.

Pairing tokens and plan drafts use Android Keystore-backed encrypted storage
excluded from ordinary configuration export/backup. Plan drafts are bounded
and expire after seven days. Re-pairing changes the identity used for drafts
and notification links. Drafts and audio are not added to T3 diagnostic logs.
Existing optional user-enabled recording settings still apply.

If device tools are enabled, the paired bridge can invoke the registered
Faceclaw tools for the configured assistant project. These can read device
state and Android notifications or perform actions authorized by the user and
Android permissions. Tool output returns to the calling T3 agent. Notification
content can appear on the phone and glasses. Proactive actions need not show an
additional confirmation when the user's consent or project rules authorize
them. Project rules do not replace authenticated transport or OS permissions.

Sample-data prompts remain local. Configured live assistant/tool connections
can continue in the background while the T3 app displays sample data.

The original upstream policy follows.

# Faceclaw Privacy Policy

Faceclaw is a privacy-conscious app. The developers of Faceclaw don't collect
or store user data. Faceclaw does integrate with some third-party services,
which have their own privacy policies; this document describes what those
services are, what they're used for and what data they collect, and links to
their privacy policies.


## Audio

Faceclaw allows users to transcribe audio (for use with the voice assistant,
the Transcribe app, typing into Terminal, and other apps). The default setting
is that this is done using an on-device model running on your phone. If an
on-device model is selected, audio never leaves your device, and is not stored
after it is processed.

Users can also provide their own API key for a third-party transcription
service such as OpenAI Whisper, ElevenLabs, or Soniox, which may offer better
accuracy.
Faceclaw never transmits audio to any party other than a user-selected
transcription service. If a third-party transcription service is selected,
audio will be sent to that third party, is linked to the account with which
that API key was created, and is subject to that party's privacy and retention
policies:

  OpenAI: https://openai.com/policies/row-privacy-policy/
  ElevenLabs: https://elevenlabs.io/privacy-policy
  Soniox: https://soniox.com/policies/privacy-policy

Third party data retention and privacy policies may differ based on the API
key provided. API keys are part of a relationship between the user and the API
provider; Faceclaw does not manage accounts or process billing information.


## AI Assistant Transcripts

Faceclaw has a builtin AI voice assistant. Using the voice assistant requires
providing an API key for a model provider such as Anthropic or OpenAI. Prompts
and responses are subject to that model provider's privacy policy:

  OpenAI: https://openai.com/policies/row-privacy-policy/
  Anthropic: https://www.anthropic.com/legal/privacy

AI conversation histories and memory files may be stored on your phone.
Faceclaw does not transmit voice-assistant prompts or responses to any party
other than a user-selected AI model provider. The voice assistant is given
tools with which it is able to access most things on the user's phone,
including all content displayed on the glasses, location, notifications, and
content accessible through the Terminal app.

The AI assistant can take actions on the user's behalf, including sharing any
information it has access to. The user is responsible for any instructions
they give it.


## Location

Faceclaw has a builtin Navigate app, which provides turn-by-turn directions.
Using the Navigate app requires providing an API key for Mapbox. While the
Navigate app is active, the user's exact location and selected destination are
transmitted to Mapbox, and are subject to Mapbox's privacy policy.

  Mapbox: https://www.mapbox.com/legal/privacy

The Weather app will retrieve the user's coarse location, and will use it to
query a third-party weather information provider.


## EvenHub

The EvenHub app store is subject to Even Realities' privacy policy:
https://support.evenrealities.com/hc/en-us/articles/14127464826511-Privacy-Policy.
Apps downloaded from the EvenHub app store come with their own privacy
policies.


## Wear OS Watch

The optional Faceclaw watch app (wear/) talks to the phone over Google Play
services' Wearable Data Layer, the same channel the watch uses for
notifications and other companion apps. Gestures, commands, the watch's own
speech-recognition results, and the mirrored glasses status and assistant
replies travel between your watch and your phone over their Bluetooth (or,
when paired that way, Wi-Fi) link; Faceclaw does not send any of it to a
server. Speech recognized on the watch uses the watch's system speech
recognizer, which may send audio to Google under Google's privacy policy:

  Google: https://policies.google.com/privacy

The phone does nothing watch-related on phones without Google Play services.
